 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
-- Since: 2026-09-02
-- Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
-- Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
-- Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
-  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
-- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+### §contiguous-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the remaining tabs
+- Since: 2026-10-23. Replaces §stable-tab-keys (since 2026-09-02), which the maintainer overrode on 2026-10-23.
+- Decision: keys are always contiguous `t1..tN` in tab order. Closing a tab renumbers the remaining tabs in their
+  current order and resets `next` to N+1, so the next open continues the sequence (`t1,t2`, then `t1,t2,t3`).
+- Forbidden: leaving gaps in the keys after a close (`t1,t3`).
+- Reason: the maintainer overrode §stable-tab-keys because they want contiguous keys. Known trade-off, carried over
+  from that record: the key is both the React key and the URL state, so renumbering remounts every tab to the right
+  of the closed one and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29; the "close keeps keys"
+  test that reproduced it was removed in this change).
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs` — "close renumbers keys", "close first tab renumbers keys", "open after close
+  continues contiguously".
